        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "`python3 check.py` now fails when code in `src` calls `eval`. I made the change by adding `\"eval\"` to `forbidden_builtins` in `checks.json`. `check.py` didn't need to change.\n\n**Testing:** I ran the check on throwaway sample files in a temp folder, plus the real repo:\n- A file containing only `# eval(x)` in a comment, `\"eval(1)\"` in a string, `obj.eval()` and `model.eval(x)` passed.\n- A file containing `eval(\"1+1\")` failed and was listed in the output.\n- The repo itself still passes, since `src/app.py` doesn't use `eval`.\n\n**What it won't catch:** the check only flags a call written directly as `eval(...)`. It misses indirect uses such as `builtins.eval(...)`, `f = eval; f(...)` or `getattr(__builtins__, \"eval\")`. Covering those would mean changing `check.py` itself. I can do that if you want stricter coverage."
        }
